{"id":"gen-1790978544-GSNcktIPP1RK5UsoKlzg","object":"chat.completion","created":1790978544,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-shared-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 1,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 1,\n        \"e\": 2,\n        \"f\": 2,\n        \"g\": 2,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 2,\n        \"k\": 3,\n        \"l\": 0,\n        \"m\": 3,\n        \"n\": 2,\n        \"o\": 3,\n        \"p\": 3\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c1-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"shared-integrator\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a CSP problem. The problem defines a set of variables a-p each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 (binary constraints) and also constraints c17..c20 (also binary constraints but with only two allowed pairs each). There are also component definitions linking variables to components c1..c4. The claims c1-tuples, c2-tuples, c3-tuples provide some tuples for components c1, c2, c3. Those claims are presumably evidence that the assignment must satisfy those component tuples? The problem statement: \"Return a complete assignment of all sixteen variables satisfying the public CSP.\" The shared integrator must solve using only the public facts. The claims provide some tuples for components c1, c2, c3. The claims are presumably considered evidence that those component values must be as given? However, the claims are not constraints but \"claims\" that have been posted. The integrator's job is to produce a solution that satisfies all constraints (c1..c20) and also aligns with the claims? The claims provide specific values for variables in components c1, c2, c3. It says \"c1-tuples\" value: component: c1, variables: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"], tuples: [[1,0,3,1]]. So this claim says that a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1. Similarly c2-tuples: e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0. c3-tuples: i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0. Those are presumably known facts that must be part of the solution. The integrator must produce an assignment that satisfies all constraints and also matches these claim values. It also must satisfy the constraints c17..c20 which involve variables across components. So we need to find a consistent assignment for a-p that satisfies:\n\n- All binary constraints c1..c20 (including c1-c16 constraints with 6 allowed pairs each, and c17-c20 with 2 allowed pairs each)\n- The values from claims: a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1; e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0; i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nThus we need to verify that those claim values satisfy all constraints. If not, we need to see if there are alternative assignments that satisfy all constraints but also match the claim values? Usually claims are evidence that the assignment must satisfy those values. So we must produce an assignment that matches them. If they conflict with constraints, then there is no solution, but presumably there is one.\n\nThus we need to check constraints.\n\nFirst, list variables and their claimed values:\n\n- a = 1\n- b = 0\n- c = 3\n- d = 1\n- e = 2\n- f = 2\n- g = 2\n- h = 0\n- i = 0\n- j = 2\n- k = 3\n- l = 0\n- m = ?\n- n = ?\n- o = ?\n- p = ?\n\nWe need to assign m,n,o,p to values in {0,1,2,3} such that all constraints are satisfied, including constraints that involve these variables.\n\nNow list constraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b allowed pairs: [[1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3]].\n\nOur a=1, b=0. Pair (1,0) is allowed (first pair). So c1 satisfied.\n\nc2: variables b,c allowed pairs: [[0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2]].\n\nOur b=0, c=3 -> pair (0,3) allowed (first pair). So c2 satisfied.\n\nc3: variables c,d allowed pairs: [[3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1]].\n\nOur c=3, d=1 -> pair (3,1) allowed (first pair). So c3 satisfied.\n\nc4: variables d,a allowed pairs: [[1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2]].\n\nOur d=1, a=1 -> pair (1,1) allowed (first pair). So c4 satisfied.\n\nc5: variables e,f allowed pairs: [[2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0]].\n\nOur e=2, f=2 -> pair (2,2) allowed (first pair). So c5 satisfied.\n\nc6: variables f,g allowed pairs: [[2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3]].\n\nOur f=2, g=2 -> pair (2,2) allowed (first pair). So c6 satisfied.\n\nc7: variables g,h allowed pairs: [[2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1]].\n\nOur g=2, h=0 -> pair (2,0) allowed (first pair). So c7 satisfied.\n\nc8: variables h,e allowed pairs: [[0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1]].\n\nOur h=0, e=2 -> pair (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c8 satisfied.\n\nc9: variables i,j allowed pairs: [[0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3]].\n\nOur i=0, j=2 -> pair (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c9 satisfied.\n\nc10: variables j,k allowed pairs: [[2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0]].\n\nOur j=2, k=3 -> pair (2,3) allowed (first pair). So c10 satisfied.\n\nc11: variables k,l allowed pairs: [[3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3]].\n\nOur k=3, l=0 -> pair (3,0) allowed (first pair). So c11 satisfied.\n\nc12: variables l,i allowed pairs: [[0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3]].\n\nOur l=0, i=0 -> pair (0,0) allowed (first pair). So c12 satisfied.\n\nc13: variables m,n allowed pairs: [[3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2]].\n\nWe haven't assigned m,n yet; must satisfy this.\n\nc14: variables n,o allowed pairs: [[2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2]].\n\nc15: variables o,p allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2]].\n\nc16: variables p,m allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0]].\n\nc17: variables b,e allowed pairs: [[0,2], [0,3]].\n\nOur b=0, e=2 -> (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c17 satisfied.\n\nc18: variables f,i allowed pairs: [[2,0], [3,1]].\n\nOur f=2, i=0 -> (2,0) allowed (first pair). So c18 satisfied.\n\nc19: variables j,m allowed pairs: [[2,3], [0,0]].\n\nOur j=2, unknown m; allowed pairs: (2,3) or (0,0). So m must be 3 if j=2 (since (2,3) allowed) or if j=0 then m=0. But j=2, so m must be 3. So m=3.\n\nc20: variables n,a allowed pairs: [[2,1], [3,3]].\n\nOur a=1, n unknown; allowed pairs: (2,1) or (3,3). So (n,a) must be (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, the second component is a=1. So possible pairs: (2,1) => n=2; (3,3) => n=3. So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nThus we have m=3.\n\nNow we need to assign n, o, p consistent with constraints c13-c16 and also c20 (n,a) and c19 (j,m) already satisfied.\n\nNow we have m=3.\n\nConstraint c13: m,n allowed pairs: [[3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2]].\n\nWe have m=3. So allowed n values for m=3 are from pairs where first is 3: (3,2) -> n=2; (3,3) -> n=3. Also (3,?) maybe other? The list includes (3,2) and (3,3). So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nConstraint c20: n must be 2 or 3 (as above). So consistent. So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nNow c14: n,o allowed pairs: [[2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2]].\n\nWe need to assign o based on n.\n\nc15: o,p allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2]].\n\nc16: p,m allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0]].\n\nWe have m=3. So c16 constraints: p,m must be one of allowed pairs: (3,3) => p=3; (1,0) => p=1,m=0 (not match); (0,2) => p=0,m=2 (not match); (1,1) => p=1,m=1 (not match); (0,1) => p=0,m=1 (not match); (3,0) => p=3,m=0 (not match). Since m=3, only allowed pair is (3,3) giving p=3. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow c15: o,p allowed pairs: we have p=3. Allowed pairs: (3,3) => o=3; (1,0) => o=1,p=0 (no); (0,0) => o=0,p=0 (no); (0,1) => o=0,p=1 (no); (1,3) => o=1,p=3 (possible: o=1,p=3); (3,2) => o=3,p=2 (no). So allowed possibilities for (o,p=3) are (3,3) and (1,3). So o can be 3 or 1.\n\nNow c14: n,o allowed pairs. Let's consider n possibilities.\n\nCase 1: n=2.\n\nc14: n=2, allowed o values: pairs: (2,3) => o=3; (2,2) => o=2; (2,?) also (2,?) from list: (2,3) and (2,2). Also (2,?) maybe (2,?) from (2,?) we have (2,3) and (2,2). So o can be 3 or 2.\n\nBut from c15 we have o ∈ {3,1}. Intersection: o can be 3 only (since 2 not allowed). So if n=2, o must be 3.\n\nNow check c13: n=2 is allowed with m=3 (pair (3,2) is allowed). So n=2 works.\n\nThus assignment: n=2, o=3, p=3.\n\nCheck all constraints: c13 satisfied (m=3,n=2). c14 satisfied (n=2,o=3). c15 satisfied (o=3,p=3). c16 satisfied (p=3,m=3). c20 satisfied (n=2,a=1) is (2,1) allowed. So all constraints satisfied.\n\nCase 2: n=3.\n\nc14: n=3, allowed o values: pairs: (3,2) => o=2; (3,?) also (3,?) maybe (3,?) from list: (3,2) only. Also (3,?) other? List includes (3,2) only. So o must be 2.\n\nBut c15: o must be 3 or 1. Intersection with o=2 is empty. So n=3 impossible.\n\nThus n must be 2, o=3, p=3.\n\nThus we have full assignment:\n\na=1\nb=0\nc=3\nd=1\ne=2\nf=2\ng=2\nh=0\ni=0\nj=2\nk=3\nl=0\nm=3\nn=2\no=3\np=3\n\nNow we should double-check all constraints again thoroughly to ensure no oversight.\n\nList all constraints with given values:\n\nc1: a,b = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) present.\n\nc2: b,c = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) present.\n\nc3: c,d = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) present.\n\nc4: d,a = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) present.\n\nc5: e,f = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) present.\n\nc6: f,g = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) present.\n\nc7: g,h = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) present.\n\nc8: h,e = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc9: i,j = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc10: j,k = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc11: k,l = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) present.\n\nc12: l,i = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) present.\n\nc13: m,n = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) present.\n\nc14: n,o = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc15: o,p = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) present.\n\nc16: p,m = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) present.\n\nc17: b,e = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc18: f,i = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) present.\n\nc19: j,m = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc20: n,a = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) present.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the assignment is valid.\n\nNow the problem also mentions \"bridges\" between components: c17: b,e; c18: f,i; c19: j,m; c20: n,a. Already considered.\n\nThus the solution is found.\n\nNow we need to output JSON matching the envelope template with assignment values. The envelope template shows fields: \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-shared-integrator-1\", \"payload\": {\"conclusion\": {\"assignment\": {...}}}, \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\", \"read_refs\": [ ...], \"recipients\": [\"shared-integrator\"], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"shared-integrator\". The \"assignment\" object should have keys a-p with integer values.\n\nThus we need to produce exactly that JSON.\n\nOne nuance: The problem says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output the envelope JSON with assignment values as we found.\n\nThus final answer is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-shared-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 1,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 1,\n        \"e\": 2,\n        \"f\": 2,\n        \"g\": 2,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 2,\n        \"k\": 3,\n        \"l\": 0,\n        \"m\": 3,\n        \"n\": 2,\n        \"o\": 3,\n        \"p\": 3\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c1-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"shared-integrator\"\n}\n\nCheck if any missing fields? The template includes \"read_refs\" with those four entries. Good.\n\nThus output exactly that.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a CSP problem. The problem defines a set of variables a-p each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c16 (binary constraints) and also constraints c17..c20 (also binary constraints but with only two allowed pairs each). There are also component definitions linking variables to components c1..c4. The claims c1-tuples, c2-tuples, c3-tuples provide some tuples for components c1, c2, c3. Those claims are presumably evidence that the assignment must satisfy those component tuples? The problem statement: \"Return a complete assignment of all sixteen variables satisfying the public CSP.\" The shared integrator must solve using only the public facts. The claims provide some tuples for components c1, c2, c3. The claims are presumably considered evidence that those component values must be as given? However, the claims are not constraints but \"claims\" that have been posted. The integrator's job is to produce a solution that satisfies all constraints (c1..c20) and also aligns with the claims? The claims provide specific values for variables in components c1, c2, c3. It says \"c1-tuples\" value: component: c1, variables: [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"], tuples: [[1,0,3,1]]. So this claim says that a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1. Similarly c2-tuples: e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0. c3-tuples: i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0. Those are presumably known facts that must be part of the solution. The integrator must produce an assignment that satisfies all constraints and also matches these claim values. It also must satisfy the constraints c17..c20 which involve variables across components. So we need to find a consistent assignment for a-p that satisfies:\n\n- All binary constraints c1..c20 (including c1-c16 constraints with 6 allowed pairs each, and c17-c20 with 2 allowed pairs each)\n- The values from claims: a=1, b=0, c=3, d=1; e=2, f=2, g=2, h=0; i=0, j=2, k=3, l=0.\n\nThus we need to verify that those claim values satisfy all constraints. If not, we need to see if there are alternative assignments that satisfy all constraints but also match the claim values? Usually claims are evidence that the assignment must satisfy those values. So we must produce an assignment that matches them. If they conflict with constraints, then there is no solution, but presumably there is one.\n\nThus we need to check constraints.\n\nFirst, list variables and their claimed values:\n\n- a = 1\n- b = 0\n- c = 3\n- d = 1\n- e = 2\n- f = 2\n- g = 2\n- h = 0\n- i = 0\n- j = 2\n- k = 3\n- l = 0\n- m = ?\n- n = ?\n- o = ?\n- p = ?\n\nWe need to assign m,n,o,p to values in {0,1,2,3} such that all constraints are satisfied, including constraints that involve these variables.\n\nNow list constraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b allowed pairs: [[1,0], [1,3], [0,2], [2,3], [0,1], [0,3]].\n\nOur a=1, b=0. Pair (1,0) is allowed (first pair). So c1 satisfied.\n\nc2: variables b,c allowed pairs: [[0,3], [1,1], [1,3], [1,2], [3,3], [0,2]].\n\nOur b=0, c=3 -> pair (0,3) allowed (first pair). So c2 satisfied.\n\nc3: variables c,d allowed pairs: [[3,1], [0,1], [0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [1,1]].\n\nOur c=3, d=1 -> pair (3,1) allowed (first pair). So c3 satisfied.\n\nc4: variables d,a allowed pairs: [[1,1], [2,1], [1,2], [2,2], [2,0], [3,2]].\n\nOur d=1, a=1 -> pair (1,1) allowed (first pair). So c4 satisfied.\n\nc5: variables e,f allowed pairs: [[2,2], [1,2], [3,1], [1,3], [1,0], [2,0]].\n\nOur e=2, f=2 -> pair (2,2) allowed (first pair). So c5 satisfied.\n\nc6: variables f,g allowed pairs: [[2,2], [1,1], [0,0], [1,3], [0,2], [0,3]].\n\nOur f=2, g=2 -> pair (2,2) allowed (first pair). So c6 satisfied.\n\nc7: variables g,h allowed pairs: [[2,0], [3,2], [1,1], [2,1], [1,3], [3,1]].\n\nOur g=2, h=0 -> pair (2,0) allowed (first pair). So c7 satisfied.\n\nc8: variables h,e allowed pairs: [[0,2], [1,0], [1,3], [2,3], [3,0], [2,1]].\n\nOur h=0, e=2 -> pair (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c8 satisfied.\n\nc9: variables i,j allowed pairs: [[0,2], [2,0], [0,3], [3,2], [2,1], [2,3]].\n\nOur i=0, j=2 -> pair (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c9 satisfied.\n\nc10: variables j,k allowed pairs: [[2,3], [0,0], [1,1], [2,2], [1,3], [3,0]].\n\nOur j=2, k=3 -> pair (2,3) allowed (first pair). So c10 satisfied.\n\nc11: variables k,l allowed pairs: [[3,0], [0,3], [1,2], [1,0], [3,1], [3,3]].\n\nOur k=3, l=0 -> pair (3,0) allowed (first pair). So c11 satisfied.\n\nc12: variables l,i allowed pairs: [[0,0], [3,3], [2,1], [2,0], [0,3], [1,3]].\n\nOur l=0, i=0 -> pair (0,0) allowed (first pair). So c12 satisfied.\n\nc13: variables m,n allowed pairs: [[3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2]].\n\nWe haven't assigned m,n yet; must satisfy this.\n\nc14: variables n,o allowed pairs: [[2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2]].\n\nc15: variables o,p allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2]].\n\nc16: variables p,m allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0]].\n\nc17: variables b,e allowed pairs: [[0,2], [0,3]].\n\nOur b=0, e=2 -> (0,2) allowed (first pair). So c17 satisfied.\n\nc18: variables f,i allowed pairs: [[2,0], [3,1]].\n\nOur f=2, i=0 -> (2,0) allowed (first pair). So c18 satisfied.\n\nc19: variables j,m allowed pairs: [[2,3], [0,0]].\n\nOur j=2, unknown m; allowed pairs: (2,3) or (0,0). So m must be 3 if j=2 (since (2,3) allowed) or if j=0 then m=0. But j=2, so m must be 3. So m=3.\n\nc20: variables n,a allowed pairs: [[2,1], [3,3]].\n\nOur a=1, n unknown; allowed pairs: (2,1) or (3,3). So (n,a) must be (2,1) or (3,3). Since a=1, the second component is a=1. So possible pairs: (2,1) => n=2; (3,3) => n=3. So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nThus we have m=3.\n\nNow we need to assign n, o, p consistent with constraints c13-c16 and also c20 (n,a) and c19 (j,m) already satisfied.\n\nNow we have m=3.\n\nConstraint c13: m,n allowed pairs: [[3,2], [2,2], [3,3], [1,1], [0,2], [1,2]].\n\nWe have m=3. So allowed n values for m=3 are from pairs where first is 3: (3,2) -> n=2; (3,3) -> n=3. Also (3,?) maybe other? The list includes (3,2) and (3,3). So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nConstraint c20: n must be 2 or 3 (as above). So consistent. So n can be 2 or 3.\n\nNow c14: n,o allowed pairs: [[2,3], [3,2], [1,0], [1,2], [0,3], [2,2]].\n\nWe need to assign o based on n.\n\nc15: o,p allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2]].\n\nc16: p,m allowed pairs: [[3,3], [1,0], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0]].\n\nWe have m=3. So c16 constraints: p,m must be one of allowed pairs: (3,3) => p=3; (1,0) => p=1,m=0 (not match); (0,2) => p=0,m=2 (not match); (1,1) => p=1,m=1 (not match); (0,1) => p=0,m=1 (not match); (3,0) => p=3,m=0 (not match). Since m=3, only allowed pair is (3,3) giving p=3. So p must be 3.\n\nThus p=3.\n\nNow c15: o,p allowed pairs: we have p=3. Allowed pairs: (3,3) => o=3; (1,0) => o=1,p=0 (no); (0,0) => o=0,p=0 (no); (0,1) => o=0,p=1 (no); (1,3) => o=1,p=3 (possible: o=1,p=3); (3,2) => o=3,p=2 (no). So allowed possibilities for (o,p=3) are (3,3) and (1,3). So o can be 3 or 1.\n\nNow c14: n,o allowed pairs. Let's consider n possibilities.\n\nCase 1: n=2.\n\nc14: n=2, allowed o values: pairs: (2,3) => o=3; (2,2) => o=2; (2,?) also (2,?) from list: (2,3) and (2,2). Also (2,?) maybe (2,?) from (2,?) we have (2,3) and (2,2). So o can be 3 or 2.\n\nBut from c15 we have o ∈ {3,1}. Intersection: o can be 3 only (since 2 not allowed). So if n=2, o must be 3.\n\nNow check c13: n=2 is allowed with m=3 (pair (3,2) is allowed). So n=2 works.\n\nThus assignment: n=2, o=3, p=3.\n\nCheck all constraints: c13 satisfied (m=3,n=2). c14 satisfied (n=2,o=3). c15 satisfied (o=3,p=3). c16 satisfied (p=3,m=3). c20 satisfied (n=2,a=1) is (2,1) allowed. So all constraints satisfied.\n\nCase 2: n=3.\n\nc14: n=3, allowed o values: pairs: (3,2) => o=2; (3,?) also (3,?) maybe (3,?) from list: (3,2) only. Also (3,?) other? List includes (3,2) only. So o must be 2.\n\nBut c15: o must be 3 or 1. Intersection with o=2 is empty. So n=3 impossible.\n\nThus n must be 2, o=3, p=3.\n\nThus we have full assignment:\n\na=1\nb=0\nc=3\nd=1\ne=2\nf=2\ng=2\nh=0\ni=0\nj=2\nk=3\nl=0\nm=3\nn=2\no=3\np=3\n\nNow we should double-check all constraints again thoroughly to ensure no oversight.\n\nList all constraints with given values:\n\nc1: a,b = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) present.\n\nc2: b,c = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) present.\n\nc3: c,d = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) present.\n\nc4: d,a = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) present.\n\nc5: e,f = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) present.\n\nc6: f,g = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) present.\n\nc7: g,h = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) present.\n\nc8: h,e = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc9: i,j = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc10: j,k = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc11: k,l = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) present.\n\nc12: l,i = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) present.\n\nc13: m,n = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) present.\n\nc14: n,o = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc15: o,p = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) present.\n\nc16: p,m = (3,3) allowed? Yes (3,3) present.\n\nc17: b,e = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) present.\n\nc18: f,i = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) present.\n\nc19: j,m = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) present.\n\nc20: n,a = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) present.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the assignment is valid.\n\nNow the problem also mentions \"bridges\" between components: c17: b,e; c18: f,i; c19: j,m; c20: n,a. Already considered.\n\nThus the solution is found.\n\nNow we need to output JSON matching the envelope template with assignment values. The envelope template shows fields: \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-shared-integrator-1\", \"payload\": {\"conclusion\": {\"assignment\": {...}}}, \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\", \"read_refs\": [ ...], \"recipients\": [\"shared-integrator\"], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"shared-integrator\". The \"assignment\" object should have keys a-p with integer values.\n\nThus we need to produce exactly that JSON.\n\nOne nuance: The problem says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output the envelope JSON with assignment values as we found.\n\nThus final answer is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"CONCLUDE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6412-shared-integrator-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"assignment\": {\n        \"a\": 1,\n        \"b\": 0,\n        \"c\": 3,\n        \"d\": 1,\n        \"e\": 2,\n        \"f\": 2,\n        \"g\": 2,\n        \"h\": 0,\n        \"i\": 0,\n        \"j\": 2,\n        \"k\": 3,\n        \"l\": 0,\n        \"m\": 3,\n        \"n\": 2,\n        \"o\": 3,\n        \"p\": 3\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6412\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c1-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c2-tuple